@startuml
title Implemented S1-S4 domain lifecycle — immutable State behavior
actor "Domain caller / test" as Caller
participant "pending : Appointment" as Original
participant DomainTime as Time
participant PendingState as Pending
participant "confirmed : Appointment" as Confirmed
participant ConfirmedState as State
Caller -> Original : confirm(time)
Original -> Time : now()
Time --> Original : injected Clock instant
Original -> Pending : confirm(slot, now)
alt now < start
  Pending --> Original : new ConfirmedState
  Original --> Caller : new confirmed appointment of same subtype
  note over Original, Confirmed
    Original remains PENDING; caller retains returned value.
    Mode family and enhancement snapshot are preserved.
    Domain calls alone do not commit; repository persists replacements.
S4 records revisions/events atomically, then delivers after unlock.
  end note
else now >= start
  Pending --> Original : DomainValidationException
  Original --> Caller : failure, original unchanged
end
Caller -> Confirmed : complete(time)
Confirmed -> Time : now()
Time --> Confirmed : injected Clock instant
Confirmed -> State : complete(slot, now)
alt now >= end
  State --> Confirmed : new CompletedState
  Confirmed --> Caller : new completed appointment of same subtype
else now < end
  State --> Confirmed : DomainValidationException
  Confirmed --> Caller : failure, confirmed unchanged
end
note over Confirmed
validateReschedule delegates eligibility to State,
validates proposed slot, and returns TimeSlot only.
S3 reschedule uses that proposal to create a same-State/subtype replacement.
Repository commits after atomic availability checks; see scheduling sequence.
COMPLETED/CANCELLED reject all lifecycle operations.
end note
@enduml
